etting of acute abdominal pain: temporal trends and appropriateness of requesting</article-title><source>Ann R Coll Surg Engl</source><year>2006</year><volume>88</volume><fpage>270</fpage><lpage>274</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1308/003588406X98586</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16719997</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="CR4"><label>4.</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>van Randen</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Lam&#x000e9;ris</surname><given-names>W</given-names></name><name><surname>Luitse</surname><given-names>JSK</given-names></name><name><surname>Gorzeman</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Hesselink</surname><given-names>EJ</given-names></name><name><surname>DEJGJ</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>The role of plain radiographs in patients with acute abdominal pain at the ED</article-title><source>Am J Emerg Med</source><year>2011</year><volume>29</volume><fpage>582</fpage><lpage>9.e2</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/j.ajem.2009.12.020</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">20825832</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="CR5"><label>5.</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Jackson</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Taylor</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Judkins</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Emergency department abdominal x-rays have a poor diagnostic yield and their usefulness is questionable</article-title><source>Emerg Med J</source><year>2011</year><volume>28</volume><fpage>745</fpage><lpage>749</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1136/emj.2010.094730</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">20713362</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="CR6"><label>6.</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><